              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Learn the first body part with its article and its characteristic final sound."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: der Kopf",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "der Kopf — the head."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is masculine and capitalised because every German noun begins with a capital letter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need: Make the -pf sequence",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "At the end of Kopf, close for p and release directly into f. English has no identical native ending, so practise it slowly:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ko-p-f becomes Kopf."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: Kopf + Schmerzen",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A useful phrase is:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ich habe Kopfschmerzen. — I have a headache."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "German compounds simply place nouns together: Kopf (head) + Schmerzen (pains)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
