    {
      "id": "GE-C17-hand",
      "sequence": 680,
      "title": "die Hand — \"the hand\"",
      "headword": "die Hand",
      "romanization": "die Hand",
      "gloss": "the hand — the same word as English, and unrelated to Romance manus",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:542e3aa2402318b7",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "After the tangle of Kopf and Haupt, here is the easiest word in the chapter — and a useful lesson about when languages don't match."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: die Hand",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "die Hand — the hand. Feminine, and spelled exactly like English."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need: Final devoicing",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One pronunciation note: the final d is said as a t. German devoices a consonant at the end of a word, so Hand ends like English hunt — but with the open ah of Tag, not hunt's vowel. Add an ending and the d comes back: die Hände (\"the hands\")."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: It is the English word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hand is Germanic handuz, and English inherited it directly. Same word, same meaning, two thousand years apart. German and English are close relatives, and body parts are where you feel it most:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "German",
                "English"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 5,
              "utterances": [
                "Hand means hand.",
                "Arm means arm.",
                "Finger means finger.",
                "Fuß means foot.",
                "Herz means heart."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"die Hand\" — final d said as t",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"die Hand\" — final **d** said as **t**]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the plural, and hear the d return — \"die Hände\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the plural, and hear the *d* return — \"die Hän**d**e\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the Germanic set — \"Hand, Arm, Finger, Fuß, Herz\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the Germanic set — \"Hand, Arm, Finger, Fuß, Herz\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the gap — \"main, mano, mão … but Hand\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the gap — \"*main*, *mano*, *mão* … but **Hand**\"]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"the hand.\" (Die Hand — feminine.) How is the final d pronounced, and why? (As a t — German devoices final consonants; the d returns in Hände.) Is Hand related to French main? (No — Germanic handuz against Latin manus; the families differ here.) How did manus reach German anyway? (As borrowed learned words — Manuskript, manuell.) Next: the rest of the body."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "GE-C17-hand-more",
      "sequence": 683,
      "title": "(die Hand, continued) — Where the family tree stops",
      "headword": "(die Hand, continued)",
      "romanization": "(die Hand, continued)",
      "gloss": "Where the family tree stops",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
